data: {"id":"chatcmpl-8101e3b7-71fd-4376-a0ac-8c56b2757e28","object":"chat.completion.chunk","created":1790744767,"model":"capitoline-judge-sol6","choices":[{"index":0,"delta":{},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":44582,"completion_tokens":3801,"total_tokens":48383},"capitoline":{"provider":"capitoline","ignored":[],"council":{"deliberationId":"01bf5f29-3039-48d9-a330-cecf29ca7009","strategyVersion":2,"shape":"fast","members":[{"family":"anthropic","model":"claude-opus","label":"Response D","answer":"**Immediate effect:** RF2 keeps two copies of every piece of data on two different nodes. When a node fails, any data that had a copy on that node now has only one surviving copy. That data is still available, but it is **not redundant** until it is rebuilt. VMs from the failed host restart on the surviving nodes through AHV HA, provided there is enough memory.\n\n**What the cluster does:** Curator detects the missing replicas and starts re-replicating them. The work is spread across the CVMs on the two remaining nodes, so every extent again has copies on two different nodes. Two nodes are enough to restore RF2, but only if there is enough free capacity. That is why Nutanix recommends reserving about one node's worth of storage as headroom.\n\n**Caveats:**\n- Metadata (Cassandra) and Zookeeper keep quorum with 2 of 3 nodes, so the cluster stays up.\n- A second node failure would break quorum, even after the data rebuild finishes.\n- Full resilience returns only when the node is repaired or replaced.\n- How long the rebuild takes depends on data volume and hardware.","fellBackFrom":["claude-fable (rate_limited)"]},{"family":"openai","model":"codex-gpt-6-astra","label":"Response C","answer":"In an RF2 cluster, Nutanix normally keeps **two copies of each data block on different nodes**. If one of three nodes fails, blocks that had a copy on that node are left with only one accessible copy. Blocks stored on the two surviving nodes still have both copies. The cluster can continue serving data, and affected VMs can restart on surviving nodes if sufficient compute resources are available.\n\nNutanix can **re-replicate the affected data across the two surviving nodes**, provided there is enough usable storage capacity. Thus, losing a node does not necessarily mean the data must remain single-copy until that node returns.\n\nHowever, restoring two data copies is **not the same as restoring full cluster failure tolerance**. With only two nodes available, the cluster cannot safely tolerate another node failure: metadata and quorum requirements still matter. Repairing or replacing the failed node restores the three-node configuration needed to regain normal protection."},{"family":"google","model":"antigravity-gemini-pro","label":"Response B","answer":"In a three-node Nutanix cluster running Replication Factor 2 (RF2), data is stored with two copies across the cluster. If one node fails, the data redundancy for the storage blocks residing on that node drops to RF1, meaning those specific blocks temporarily have no backup copy. \n\nThe cluster metadata (Cassandra/Zookeeper) maintains quorum because two out of three nodes survive, allowing storage I/O to continue normally. Nutanix High Availability (HA) will restart the failed node's VMs on the surviving nodes.\n\nTo address the degraded redundancy, the cluster automatically initiates a self-healing process. The Curator service scans for the missing data and rebuilds the lost replicas onto the remaining two nodes to fully restore RF2, provided there is enough free storage capacity available. While RF2 can be restored on the two nodes, the cluster remains in a critical state: it cannot survive a second node failure without losing metadata quorum (which halts cluster I/O) and cannot undergo upgrades until a third node is restored.\n"},{"family":"open-weights","model":"antigravity-gpt-oss","label":"Response A","answer":"In a Nutanix AOS cluster the **Replication Factor 2 (RF2)** means that every data block is stored on **two different nodes**.  \nIn a three‑node cluster there are exactly two copies of each block spread across the three servers. When one node crashes the cluster loses whichever copy(s) that happened to reside on that node, but the other two nodes still hold **at least one copy of every block**.  \n\nConsequences:\n\n* **Redundancy drops from “2‑of‑3” to “1‑of‑2”** – the cluster can no longer guarantee a second replica for any given block until it rebuilds the missing copies.  \n* The cluster **remains online** and continues to serve reads and writes (writes are temporarily written only to the surviving nodes).  \n* A **re‑balance/re‑replication process** is automatically started. Nutanix copies the missing replicas onto the remaining nodes, using any free space they have, so that RF2 is restored once the rebuild finishes.  \n\nDuring this period the cluster is **degraded**: performance may be lower and a second node failure would cause data loss. After the rebuild completes, full RF2 redundancy is restored.\n"}],"lost":[],"rankings":[],"aggregate":[],"judge":{"model":"codex-gpt-6-sol","blind":true},"calls":5}}}

